# Cyrius Dependency Management — Vidya Reference
# How cyrius.toml deps work, stdlib vs project deps, and the dep resolution flow.
# 2026-04-15

[[entries]]
name = "cyrius_toml_overview"
description = "cyrius.cyml is the project manifest — declares build config, stdlib deps, and external deps (legacy cyrius.toml is a fallback)"
category = "cyrius"
---
Every Cyrius project has a cyrius.cyml at the root (CYML = TOML header +
markdown body, v5.0.0+). Legacy cyrius.toml is still read as a fallback.
Three sections matter for deps:

    [package]
    name = "myproject"
    version = "0.1.0"

    [deps]
    stdlib = ["string", "fmt", "alloc", "io", "vec", "str", "syscalls", "assert"]

    [deps.sakshi]
    git = "https://github.com/MacCracken/sakshi.git"
    tag = "2.2.3"
    modules = ["dist/sakshi.cyr"]

The [deps] section has two forms:
1. stdlib = [...] — copies modules from the installed Cyrius stdlib into lib/
2. [deps.NAME] — clones a git repo at a tag and copies specific module files

Running `cyrius deps` resolves both. All resolved files land in lib/ as
absolute symlinks (falls back to copy). The compiler auto-includes them
via the dep include list. Namespaced deps map to lib/{depname}_{basename};
if the basename matches depname, lib/{depname}.cyr alias is also created.

[[entries]]
name = "deps_lever1_granularity"
description = "dependency-model lever 1 (Phases A-D): requires key, .deps sidecar, [groups], distlib --modular per-module extraction, and the v6.3.32 built-in std default group that dissolves the stdlib category"
category = "cyrius"
---
v6.2.46–.50 added "lever 1" of the dependency model — declaring + resolving
deps at module granularity instead of hand-ordering whole folds. Five pieces:

1. requires (v6.2.46) — a named dep declares the stdlib leaves it needs:

    [deps.sigil]
    git = "..."
    tag = "3.9.5"
    requires = ["ct", "keccak", "random"]

   `cyrius deps` pulls each leaf through the recursive stdlib resolver AHEAD
   of the dep's own modules (topological by resolution order), dedups, and
   FAILS LOUD on a missing leaf — replacing the old "ct must precede sigil"
   hand-ordering + its omit-one → runtime ud2/SIGILL trap.

2. .deps sidecar (v6.2.47/.48) — `cyrius distlib` emits dist/<pkg>.deps next
   to the fold, listing the lib/ leaves it strips at fold time (the fold
   destroys its own includes). `cyrius deps` auto-reads it, so a consumer
   declaring just [deps.<pkg>] gets the fold's stdlib in scope — no requires
   needed. The producer scans its umbrella include-hub (src/lib.cyr /
   src/main.cyr) AND excludes its own [deps.NAME] folds (those resolve
   transitively — listing them would double-include). As of v6.4.48,
   `cyrius distlib <profile>` SUBSETS the per-profile `.deps` sidecar: a
   `[lib.<profile>]` fold's `.deps` now lists only its OWN referenced
   stdlib leaves, not the whole bundle's chain (e.g. a `sha` profile no
   longer drags the full crypto suite's deps).

   v6.5.10 — the BASE bundle's sidecar is now the manifest's declared
   `[deps] stdlib` UNIONED with the include-scan, not the scan alone. The
   scan only ever saw literal `include "lib/X.cyr"` lines, so it captured
   what a module INCLUDES and never what it REFERENCES through the
   declaration (setu emitted 8 leaves against a declared 12; `result`,
   `net`, `chrono`, `args` were all referenced at top level and missed).
   That matters beyond documentation: `cyrius deps` VALIDATES a consumer's
   `[deps] stdlib` against the dep's sidecar, so an under-reporting sidecar
   silently switched OFF that check — the same under-declaring consumer
   built `OK` against the 8-leaf sidecar and hard-errored (naming `chrono`
   and `args`) against the corrected 12-leaf one. ⚠ BASE BUNDLE ONLY: a
   profile is a narrow subset of modules, so unioning the whole declaration
   into it would OVER-report and fail a legitimately-narrow consumer.
   Profiles keep the pruned inference of v6.4.48.

   v6.5.8 — `cyrius distlib --all` regenerates every `[lib.X]` profile in
   one invocation and `--check` compares BYTES (not version strings) to
   report a stale profile. Before this, distlib did exactly one bundle per
   run and nothing could ask the manifest which profiles exist — the N+1
   ritual that nearly shipped sankoch 2.7.6's gzip fix with all nine
   sub-bundles still carrying the buggy encoder under a fresh version
   string.

3. [groups] (v6.2.49) — name a recurring set once, expand at resolve time:

    [groups]
    crypto = ["ct", "keccak", "random"]

   Referenced in [deps].stdlib or a [deps.NAME] requires. Nested (groups of
   groups), deduped, self/mutual cycles skipped. Byte-identical when absent.

4. distlib --modular (v6.2.50) — per-module extraction. Emits
   dist/<pkg>/<module>.cyr (one file per [lib].modules entry, src/ self-refs
   stripped, lib/ kept) + dist/<pkg>/index.cyml — a [modular] dep graph
   `<module> = [siblings…, "lib:<leaf>"…]`. The flat single-file distlib is
   unchanged (default).

5. modular = [...] (v6.2.50) — a consumer pulls EXACT sub-modules:

    [deps.sigil]
    path = "../sigil"
    modular = ["ed25519"]

   The resolver reads dist/sigil/index.cyml and pulls ed25519 + only its
   transitive siblings + lib leaves (topo order) into lib/sigil_ed25519.cyr —
   NOT the whole monolith.

6. std default group (v6.3.32 Phase D — lever-1 COMPLETION, dissolves the "stdlib"
   category). `std` is a BUILT-IN [groups]-style group naming the universal core
   leaves: ["string", "fmt", "alloc", "io", "vec", "str", "syscalls"] — the set
   every real program pulls (matches the `cyrius init` templates). Reference it in
   the stdlib list instead of hand-ordering the core:

    [deps]
    stdlib = ["std", "chrono", "net"]   # std expands to the 7 universals

   `std` expands via the same [groups] path (item 3), in the SAME order as the
   explicit list, so a `["std", …]` list resolves the SAME leaves as spelling the
   7 out — byte-identical, and it drops the omit-one→SIGILL order-sensitivity the
   old hand-ordered list had. A manifest that defines its OWN `[groups] std = [...]`
   OVERRIDES the built-in (registered after the manifest's [groups] parse). Manifests
   that never name `std` are unaffected. `stdlib` remains the section key (back-compat
   alias); the flagship migration is descent (18-element hand list → `["std", <9
   non-core>]`, same symbol set). This completes lever 1 (Phases A–D).

SECURITY (v6.2.51, CVE-32): sub-module / leaf / pkg names from a dep-controlled
index.cyml are validated (reject any / or ..) before any filesystem op — a
malicious index sibling like `a/../../../x` would otherwise delete an arbitrary
.cyr during automatic `cyrius deps`.

Lever 2 (v6.3.1) builds on [groups] + --modular: required-vs-optional deps,
features, and platform/target scoping. The undefined-fn reachable-call
hard-error (safe once optional refs are declarable) follows in its own slot
v6.3.2 (un-bundled once lever 2 landed + its 21/192 corpus blast radius was
measured).

[[entries]]
name = "deps_lever2_optional"
description = "v6.3.1 dependency-model lever 2: optional = true, the [features] table, --features/--no-default-features, and platform-conditional target= keys (axes combine)"
category = "cyrius"
---
v6.3.1 added "lever 2" — restricting WHICH build contexts pull a dep, on top
of lever 1's WHAT-granularity. Three axes that combine:

1. optional = true — a dep that resolves only when an active feature names it:

    [deps.wgpu]
    git = "..."
    optional = true

   With no feature naming it, it is never fetched. A plain required dep (no
   `optional`) always resolves, exactly as before.

2. [features] table (Cargo-style) — named sets that activate optional deps:

    [features]
    default = ["std-io"]
    gpu = ["wgpu"]
    win-shell = ["mabda"]

   `cyrius build --features gpu` activates wgpu; plain `cyrius build` activates
   only `default` (unless --no-default-features). A feature's members may name
   other features (recursively expanded, cycle-guarded) or optional deps; a
   `--features wgpu` naming an optional dep directly activates it too.

3. target = "<arch|os>" — resolves only on a matching build target:

    [deps.mabda]
    optional = true
    target = "windows"

   Arch axis: x86_64 / x86 / aarch64 / arm64 (vs the build's --aarch64). OS
   axis: windows / agnos / bare-metal (vs --win/--agnos/--target=*-bare-metal),
   and "linux" = a native userland build. An unrecognized string never matches.

The axes COMBINE — a dep gated out on EITHER (inactive feature OR mismatched
target) is skipped entirely: no clone, no requires-pull, no module copy, no
include push. A skipped dep is NOT marked visited, so a later transitive
*required* reference can still resolve it.

GOTCHA (the ordering hazard): `cyrius`'s dep resolver (_auto_deps) fires BEFORE
each subcommand's own flag loop, so --features/--no-default-features and the
target flags are parsed in a PRE-SCAN ahead of resolution (re-reading them in
the subcommand loop is idempotent). Wiring them only into the build loop would
run the resolver with stale state.

100% cbt-side (cbt/deps.cyr + cbt/core.cyr + cbt/cyrius.cyr) — touches no src/,
so cycc is byte-identical. A manifest with no [features]/optional/target
resolves the identical include set + order as v6.2.x. Feature unification
across transitive deps stays out of scope (the consumer manifest's [features]
drives the process-global active set).

[[entries]]
name = "stdlib_deps"
description = "stdlib deps copy .cyr files from the installed Cyrius distribution into the project lib/"
category = "cyrius"
---
stdlib = ["string", "fmt", "alloc", "io", "vec", "str", "syscalls", "assert"]

Each name maps to lib/{name}.cyr in the Cyrius installation. `cyrius deps` copies
these files into the project's lib/ directory. The compiler then auto-includes them.

Common stdlib modules and what they provide:

  string   — strlen, streq, memeq, memcpy, memset, memchr, strchr, atoi, strstr
  fmt      — fmt_int, fmt_hex, fmt_sprintf (with bufsz), fmt_printf, fmt_float
  alloc    — bump allocator (alloc_init, alloc, alloc_reset), arena allocator
             (v6.5.9: arenas take an exhaustion POLICY — ARENA_FULL_NULL (0,
             the default, unchanged behaviour) / _GROW (1, chains another
             chunk) / _SPILL (2, overflow from the global allocator) /
             _ABORT (3, die at the allocation). ⚠ GROW keeps its chunks
             across arena_reset — the bump allocator underneath has no
             free() — so a request loop converges on its high-water mark.
             v6.5.7: alloc_reset() INVALIDATES every pointer it handed out,
             which used to include the memoized default allocator's own
             vtable; that now lives outside any resettable region.
             v6.5.10: alloc_via reads the vtable inline — 15.1 → 11 ns.)
  io       — file_read_all, file_write_all, file_exists, dir_list
  vec      — dynamic array (vec_new, vec_push, vec_get, vec_pop, vec_find,
             vec_sort_by / vec_select_nth since v6.5.4)
  str      — heap-allocated strings (str_from, str_cat, str_split, str_builder)
  syscalls — Linux syscall wrappers (open, close, read, write, fork, exec, etc.)
  assert   — test assertions (assert_eq, assert, test_group, assert_summary)
  net      — TCP sockets (tcp_socket, sockaddr_in)
  thread   — threads + channels (thread_create, thread_join, chan_send;
             thread_create_detached + thread_is_done since v6.5.8 — an
             unjoined thread used to leak its whole 2 MiB stack, and
             thread_join had a lost-wakeup deadlock, ~1 join in 150k).
             ⚠ thread_is_done is valid only BEFORE thread_join — join
             consumes the handle.
  sync     — mutex / condvar (mutex_lock; peers sync_macos.cyr,
             sync_windows.cyr). The Linux mutex went three-state at
             v6.5.9: 392 → 48 ns per uncontended lock/unlock pair,
             because a 2-state futex cell cannot tell the releaser
             whether anyone is parked, so it woke the kernel on EVERY
             release.
  hashmap  — string-keyed hash table (map_new, map_set, map_get, map_has)
  bayan    — JSON / TOML / CYML / CSV / base64 / bigint / u128
             (carved from stdlib at v6.1.25; include "lib/bayan.cyr")
  tls      — TLS client via libssl.so.3 (dynlib bridge)

IMPORTANT: alloc_init() must be called before any alloc() calls. Most stdlib
modules that allocate (vec, str, hashmap, bayan) require it.

[[entries]]
name = "project_deps"
description = "Project deps clone a git repo at a pinned tag and copy specific module files into lib/"
category = "cyrius"
---
External dependencies are declared as [deps.NAME] sections in cyrius.toml:

    [deps.sigil]
    git = "https://github.com/MacCracken/sigil.git"
    tag = "3.0.1"
    modules = ["dist/sigil.cyr"]

Fields:
  git     — repository URL (validated: shell metacharacters rejected)
  tag     — git tag to clone (pinned version — no floating branches)
  modules — list of .cyr files relative to repo root to import
  path    — (optional) local path instead of git clone

Resolution flow:
1. `cyrius deps` reads the [deps.NAME] section
2. Clones to $CYRIUS_HOME/deps/{name}/{tag}/ (shallow, cached)
3. For each module in modules[]:
   - Source: {clone_dir}/{module_path}
   - Dest:   lib/{depname}_{basename}  (always namespaced)
   - Creates absolute symlink (falls back to copy if symlink fails)
4. If basename matches depname (e.g. sigil.cyr for [deps.sigil]),
   also creates lib/{depname}.cyr alias for canonical include path
5. Adds to auto-include list

Consumers include via: include "lib/sigil.cyr"
The namespaced form (lib/sigil_sigil.cyr) also works but the alias is preferred.

[[entries]]
name = "dep_bundle_pattern"
description = "Multi-file deps must bundle into a single dist/*.cyr file for distribution"
category = "cyrius"
---
Cyrius has no multi-file module system — the compiler processes a single compilation
unit (one file, with includes expanded). External deps with multiple source files
must bundle them into a single .cyr file.

The standard pattern (used by sigil, patra, yukti, mabda, sankoch):

1. Create scripts/bundle.sh that concatenates sources in dependency order
2. Strip include lines (grep -v "^include ")
3. Add a header with version, source URL, license, usage
4. Output to dist/{name}.cyr

Example bundle.sh:
    for f in src/types.cyr src/core.cyr src/api.cyr; do
        echo "# --- $(basename "$f") ---"
        grep -v "^include " "$ROOT/$f"
    done > "$ROOT/dist/mylib.cyr"

Then cyrius.toml references: modules = ["dist/mylib.cyr"]

Common gotchas when bundling:
- Duplicate variable names across files: Cyrius has no block scoping for
  if/elif, so `var x` in one elif and `var x` in another elif within the
  same function collide. Hoist to function top and reassign.
- Reserved words as variable names: `match`, `type`, `break` etc. Rename.
- Large static arrays (var buf[262144]): will overflow the 1MB output buffer.
  Heap-allocate with alloc() instead.
- File ordering: types/constants first, then low-level, then high-level, then
  public API last.

[[entries]]
name = "cyrius_update_flow"
description = "cyrius update refreshes lib/ and .cyrius-toolchain from the installed Cyrius version"
category = "cyrius"
---
`cyrius update` does two things:

1. Copies all .cyr files from the installed Cyrius stdlib into lib/
   (reads $CYRIUS_HOME/current to find the installed version)
2. Updates .cyrius-toolchain to match the installed version

This is the primary upgrade mechanism. When a new Cyrius version is installed:
  $ cyrius update

After updating, re-run `cyrius deps` to refresh external deps if needed.

The .cyrius-toolchain file pins the minimum Cyrius version for CI reproducibility.
`cyrius init` creates it from VERSION; `cyrius update` keeps it current.

As of v5.0.0, the canonical manifest is cyrius.cyml (CYML = TOML header +
markdown body); legacy cyrius.toml is still read as a fallback.

[[entries]]
name = "existing_deps_registry"
description = "Current Cyrius ecosystem deps: 12 sovereign stdlib libraries, all folded byte-identical into lib/ (0 git deps)"
category = "cyrius"
---
As of v6.5.10, the ecosystem libraries are folded
byte-identical into the stdlib (0 git deps). CURRENT FOLDS — each verified
against the header of the vendored lib/<name>.cyr, not against this list:

  sigil    3.12.2     patra    1.12.12    sankoch  2.7.6
  sakshi   2.4.8      bayan    1.4.1      ganita   1.1.0
  mabda    4.0.8      niyama   1.0.6      yantra   1.0.2
  vani     1.1.3      sandhi   1.9.10     yukti    2.3.2

(sigil 3.12.2 + patra 1.12.12: thread-local slots migrated onto cyrius's
thread_local_alloc allocator, v6.4.65. sankoch 2.7.6: the gzip/deflate
block-boundary corruption fix, v6.4.79 — fixed UPSTREAM first, then folded.
sakshi 2.4.8 + bayan 1.4.1: both folded at cyrius v6.5.8 — sakshi for the
`_sk_fmt_int` half of the `i64::MIN` formatter class, bayan for a
`json_v_obj_get` cstring-vs-`Str` key mismatch. Both fixed upstream first.)

HOW TO VERIFY A FOLD VERSION — read the header of `lib/<name>.cyr`. There
are TWO header formats and a `# Version:`-only grep silently skips one:

    lib/sigil.cyr:   # sigil.cyr -- bundled distribution
                     # Version: 3.12.2
    lib/sakshi.cyr:  # Bundled distribution of sakshi v2.4.8

sakshi is the odd one out, and that is exactly how a previous sweep left
the base logger un-bumped while reporting the fold table clean. Match both
shapes, or read the file.

TWO MORE FOLD TRAPS worth knowing before you touch a vendored lib:
  - `cyrius distlib` regenerates ONLY the main bundle. Every `[lib.<profile>]`
    sub-profile keeps the OLD code until regenerated too — sankoch 2.7.6
    shipped with all 9 sub-profiles still carrying the 2.7.5 bug.
  - `cyrius lib sync` would REVERT every fold (it now refuses, v6.4.77).

The headline version on each per-dep note below is the LIVE fold; the
prose under it is accumulated lineage and still names the release that
introduced each change.

NOTE on transitive deps — the blanket "DIRECT deps only, hand-list all
transitives" that used to sit here was superseded by lever 1: a dep's
`requires` key (v6.2.46) and the `dist/<pkg>.deps` sidecar (v6.2.47/.48,
auto-read by `_dep_read_sidecar` in cbt/deps.cyr) both pull a dep's own
stdlib leaves ahead of its modules, and `modular =` (v6.2.50) walks
`index.cyml` for transitive siblings. See the deps_lever1_granularity
entry. What is still absent is a LOCKFILE and closest-wins version
resolution — moot at the moment (0 git deps; everything is folded), and
it re-matters the day a git dep comes back.


  sakshi (2.4.8) — Tracing and error handling. Two profiles:
                     sakshi.cyr (stderr-only, minimal) and
                     sakshi_full.cyr (ring buffer, UDP, span stack).
                     Zero heap allocation. Zero external deps.

  patra (1.12.12) — Structured storage and SQL queries. Bumped at
                     cyrius v5.6.20 from 1.5.5 to 1.6.0 for blob
                     support (sit consumer). Bumped again at v5.6.40
                     from 1.6.0 to 1.8.3 — INSERT OR IGNORE syntax
                     (1.7.0), STR-keyed B+ tree indexes (1.7.1), 4KB
                     page-slab allocator + word-at-a-time _memeq256 +
                     prepared statements (`patra_prepare` /
                     `patra_exec_prepared` / `patra_query_prepared` /
                     `patra_finalize`) (1.8.2), fmt/lint/doc cleanup
                     (1.8.3). **v5.7.9 BREAKING, minor bump
                     1.8.3 → 1.9.0**: renamed `fn json_build/6` →
                     `fn patra_json_build/6` to clear the silent
                     cross-module collision with
                     `lib/json.cyr::json_build/1` (the general pairs-
                     vec utility). cyrius v5.7.9's new duplicate-fn
                     warning surfaces this kind of collision at
                     registration. Consumer migration: rename
                     `json_build(buf, max, keys, vals, types, n)`
                     calls to `patra_json_build(...)`. The 7-arg
                     `json_build_lens` is unchanged. Bundled:
                     dist/patra.cyr.

  sigil (3.12.2)  — System-wide trust verification (SHA-256/512/SHAKE,
                     Ed25519, HMAC, HKDF, TPM, AES-NI, SHA-NI).
                     Bundled: dist/sigil.cyr. v2.9.0 added HKDF +
                     SHAKE-128/256; v2.9.1 consumers use AES-NI
                     cleanly after v5.5.21 fixed SSE m128 16-byte
                     alignment; v2.9.2 added SHA-NI CPUID probe +
                     dispatch entry point; v2.9.3 landed the SHA-NI
                     compress (drop-in replacement for software
                     SHA-256, ~80x throughput win on x86_64 SHA-NI
                     hosts; surfaced by sit v0.6.4's perf review).
                     Bumped 2.9.0 → 2.9.3 at cyrius v5.6.43.

  yukti (2.3.2)  — Device abstraction layer for AGNOS.
                     Bundled: dist/yukti.cyr and dist/yukti-core.cyr
                     (kernel-safe core profile).

  mabda (4.0.8)   — GPU foundation (wgpu-native shim + native NVIDIA/nouveau backend, compute dispatch).
                     Bundled: dist/mabda.cyr. Note: 141KB+ bundle
                     requires input_buf >= 256KB (raised in v3.4.19).
                     v2.5.0 depends on the u64-keyed hashmap added
                     in cyrius v5.5.20 (map_u64_* family).

  sankoch (2.7.6) — Lossless compression (LZ4, DEFLATE, zlib, gzip).
                     Bundled: dist/sankoch.cyr. Bumped 2.0.1 → 2.0.3
                     at cyrius v5.6.35 (deflate-encoder fixes for
                     sit-tree-shaped inputs); bumped 2.0.3 → 2.1.0 at
                     cyrius v5.6.43 (DEFLATE compress perf — pre-
                     reversed dynamic Huffman codes, 8-byte word-
                     compare match extension, ring-buffer absolute-
                     position match-finder; wire-format byte-for-byte
                     identical, no API change). 2.7.6 (cyrius v6.4.79) is
                     a High/silent correctness fix: batch gzip/deflate
                     re-encoded up to 258 B at every 1 MiB block boundary,
                     so every container image over 1 MiB failed CRC-32.
                     Encoders now publish their reached offset — the
                     contract the STREAMING encoder always had. The suite
                     missed it for years because every deflate test used
                     input under 1 MiB.

  bayan (1.4.1)   — Data formats + big-int: json / toml / cyml / csv /
                     base64 / bigint (u256) / u128. CARVED out of the
                     cyrius stdlib at cyrius v6.1.25 (the 7 modules left
                     stdlib so bare-metal consumers don't drag data-format
                     code into kernel objects). FOLDED byte-identical into
                     cyrius lib/bayan.cyr (sandhi-pattern — NOT a git dep).
                     Public fns renamed bayan_* (json_parse → bayan_json_parse,
                     u256_add → bayan_u256_add, …) with legacy aliases for
                     the migration window. Opt-in: include "lib/bayan.cyr".
                     ganita (math: matrix/linalg/advanced) is the sibling
                     carve at cyrius v6.1.26.

  ganita (1.0.4)  — Linear algebra + advanced math: matrix (ganita_mat_*),
                     linalg (LU/det/inv/Cholesky/QR/least-squares/eigen/SVD),
                     and the advanced math fns (transcendental + fibonacci/
                     binomial) SPLIT from stdlib lib/math.cyr. CARVED at
                     cyrius v6.1.26 (closes the data/math stdlib carve with
                     bayan). FOLDED byte-identical into cyrius lib/ganita.cyr.
                     Renamed ganita_* with legacy aliases. Keep stdlib `math`
                     in scope (f64-exp/ln polyfills + F64 constants). Opt-in:
                     include "lib/ganita.cyr".

TO USE ANY OF THESE — they ship WITH the toolchain, so there is nothing
to fetch. Name the leaf in the stdlib list and include it:

    [deps]
    stdlib = ["std", "sigil"]

    include "lib/sigil.cyr"

Then: cyrius deps

The git-dep form is still supported and is what you want for a library
that is NOT folded into the toolchain:

    [deps.mylib]
    git = "https://github.com/MacCracken/mylib.git"
    tag = "1.0.0"
    modules = ["dist/mylib.cyr"]

Do NOT point a [deps.NAME] at a folded sibling to "get a newer version" —
that puts two copies of the same symbols in one compilation unit. To move
a fold forward, fix it UPSTREAM, version-bump, regenerate the dist
(including every sub-profile), and re-vendor.
